[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"PHP 8+ Features: A Technical Deep Dive"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP 8 introduced several significant performance and developer experience improvements. This tutorial explores key features like the JIT compiler, attributes, match expression, named arguments, and union types, providing practical examples and technical insights."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"JIT (Just-In-Time) Compiler"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The JIT compiler is a major performance enhancement in PHP 8. It compiles parts of the PHP code into machine code during runtime, leading to significant speed improvements, especially for CPU-intensive applications."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"; Enable JIT in php.ini
opcache.enable=1
opcache.jit_buffer_size=100M
opcache.jit=1235"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'opcache.jit' setting controls the JIT compilation level. '1235' is an example configuration that enables tracing and function JIT."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Attributes (Annotations)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Attributes, also known as annotations in other languages, provide a way to add metadata to classes, methods, properties, and functions. They offer a structured alternative to PHPDoc comments for defining metadata."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"#[ExampleAttribute(value: 'Some Value')]
class MyClass {
    #[ExampleAttribute]
    public function myFunction() {
        // ...
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To define an attribute, create a class with the '#[Attribute]' tag. You can then use it to annotate other code elements."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"use Attribute;

#[Attribute(Attribute::TARGET_CLASS | Attribute::TARGET_METHOD)]
class ExampleAttribute {
    public function __construct(public string $value = 'Default Value') {}
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Match Expression"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'match' expression is similar to 'switch' but provides more concise and safer syntax. It supports single-line expressions and strict type comparisons."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"$result = match($statusCode) {
    200 => 'OK',
    400 => 'Bad Request',
    404 => 'Not Found',
    default => 'Unexpected Status',
};"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'match' expression returns a value based on the first matching condition. Unlike 'switch', it uses strict type comparison ('===') and does not require 'break' statements."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Named Arguments"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Named arguments allow you to pass arguments to a function by specifying the parameter name, making code more readable and less prone to errors when dealing with functions that have many optional parameters."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"function myFunction(string $firstName, string $lastName, string $title = 'Mr.') {
    echo "$title $firstName $lastName";
}

myFunction(lastName: 'Doe', firstName: 'John'); // Output: Mr. John Doe
myFunction(firstName: 'Jane', lastName: 'Smith', title: 'Ms.'); // Output: Ms. Jane Smith"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Named arguments can be passed in any order, improving code clarity and flexibility."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Union Types"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Union types allow a parameter or return type to accept multiple different types. This provides more flexibility and expressiveness in type declarations."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"function processInput(string|int $input): string|int {
    if (is_string($input)) {
        return strtoupper($input);
    } elseif (is_int($input)) {
        return $input * 2;
    } else {
        return 'Invalid Input';
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The '|' symbol is used to separate the different types in the union type declaration. In this example, the '$input' parameter can be either a string or an integer, and the function can return either a string or an integer."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"class Number {
    private int|float $amount;

    public function setAmount(int|float $amount): void {
        $this->amount = $amount;
    }

    public function getAmount(): int|float {
        return $this->amount;
    }
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Union types can also be used for class properties and method return types, enhancing type safety and code clarity."}]
